#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "b6dd2970-3cdc-58fc-8f28-0bfdcd738d6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-593" :question/problem "The region below enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt x$ and $y=x$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid. \n\n![](images/36/362a92640ed68dfd5fa85f0338d95233003e9f8a1b059a5dffa2cda243a1411b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \t\tx & =\\sqrt x  \\[3pt]\n \t\tx-\\sqrt x & = 0 \\[3pt]\n \t\t\\sqrt x \\left(\\sqrt x - 1\\right) &= 0 \\[3pt]\n \t\t\tx& = 0, 1\n\t}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/5456bed18c03e823c8e9388571b5671f5f476f5caa1085793314b57080b2b07c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=x.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x.$\n\n![](images/51/5136903876ed5ea811ebf28e0da905b5029b2c9311527fce9d0fed8e7bd1dd46.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/c8/c850643bb1c315d0516eff55d6270078504c1c98687cfac40e0c9b4b2c771b7c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(\\sqrt{x})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nV &= {\\color{blue}V_{\\text{outer}}} - {\\color{brown}V_{\\text{inner}}} \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 [1-x]^2 - \\pi \\int_0^1 [1-\\sqrt x]^2\\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 \\left((1-x)^2 - (1-\\sqrt x)^2\\right) \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 (x^2-3x+2\\sqrt x) \\,\\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi\\left. \\left( \\dfrac {1} 3 x^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} x^2 + \\dfrac{4}{3} x^{3/2 } \\ \\right)\\right|_0^1\\[5pt] \n \t\t& =  \\pi \\left[\\left( \\dfrac {1}3 (1)^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2}(1)^2  + \\dfrac{4 }{3}(1)^{3/2 } \\right) - (0) \\right]\\[5pt]\n \t\t& =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} +\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\dfrac {\\pi}{6} .\n \t\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "b79b0d88-0b42-5223-927b-5a4023f6c329" :question/math-academy-id "e-594" :question/problem "The region enclosed by the curves $x=y^2$ and $x=2-y^2$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=-1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/e8/e8f0f999521058f3213e11bd3df67a95a6ea8604e6853d06923ffbba6cf9d17c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3c9217600a26e11614752b2ca2ac8b3aa6c12b0df9f3a8c13d2b32c5b338107.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x={2-y^2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of outer and inner solids.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={2-y^2}.$\n\n![](images/36/3696bac064f5157b5c4ab3aefe34128d2d126a0a6b586106eb1e439bc90e7df5.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,(2-{y}^{2})-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2.$\n\n![](images/f5/f59ee5966f0cb9572d00e7f7278a7462841aef6b18f4865924303ad65b50738c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,({y}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(8-8{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(8y-\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3})] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "8e436e4c-45da-528c-885b-9a094919a652" :question/math-academy-id "e-7508" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8a9dd6c0bd31ceb221d0d72e6f0238ab51760cd9a32ba92bb8bdc85429a899f.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/59/59041e6817cbf26f9de00de0ec864cea57f1a419877fe5c3203857c5488c7324.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-2x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=2x^2.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=2x^2.$\n\n![](images/07/0761073a5f7f417a07f35cb0b7c08dd0b4ea6bd84da71f3e474911ee036c546b.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,(3)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[3-2{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-2x.$\n\n![](images/9e/9ea5e5dd50dcd623532557e5365118ae5c9b950be059ba8c0069c7b36071f2c3.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,(3)-(-2x)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[3+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[3-2{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[3+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[3-2{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[3+2x]}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "d064d066-3bbe-5381-ab56-3fc407da144f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7509" :question/problem "The region enclosed by the curves $x=-y^2$ and $x = -y$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the line $x={1}.$ Find an integral that gives the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/64/6471c937fb79a2266188668ebdb698eb926655b6002f8f259410b96efcb15247.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/4d/4da6108728a226e19cea708a09381c9054b129590d2a6d2c07fbfacdc30367a5.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=-{y}^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x=-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=-y.$\n\n![](images/02/02798e08e1eb8cae41e4754cfed267401298d454ce2ef856f47f2ae87a8543ee.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(1)-(-y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=-{y}^2.$\n\n![](images/5c/5c5e2060b1321856349719987e43b3424d49b465255295b8777002d85cbe6792.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(1)-(-{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[1+y]}^{2}-{[1+{y}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521c38e0-963d-5c0c-82f3-c666e0af9dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2}-{[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535563} {:answer/id #uuid "572833d5-60e0-53a8-92f2-a28672879db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}(({e}^{x+2}+2)-({e}^{{x}^{2}}+2))\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535573} {:answer/id #uuid "81318686-d93c-58d7-b209-cc8733025493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{({e}^{x+2}+2)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535640} {:answer/id #uuid "97d82ed7-ae24-5b08-afed-504bead47649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{({e}^{{x}^{2}}+2)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535673} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc3e9da-e922-5c37-9179-45b1d4651dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddc3e9da-e922-5c37-9179-45b1d4651dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535774} :answer-field/id #uuid "2049346e-586b-5a5e-af30-da5cacb1a93c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "417976f8-e97d-5f88-9ef3-7e599b484745" :question/math-academy-id "q-124592" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae4ec1bfc4bf15f8ae4d79492389dd6a3fb5e3c504b0d99a43466fc08bd4b63e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ and $y={e}^{x+2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/74/742602117733f7e0f8fa89931ff11cdfa483c3d878a99445ec1408de6aea8f30.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={e}^{x+2}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={e}^{x+2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6d2a21d75b441ca5c7c76320aad11b4dded17052056f8828e5c93b068b9bad3.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({e}^{x+2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e84714cd64d30cbb4f73c93fc6dfdfeb12f10422f0524d754906709a9a26ff6.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({e}^{{x}^{2}})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fbe30c-ec79-58b6-8bee-4aaf26e7c91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({[8x-{x}^{4}]}^{2}-1)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535448} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb28b7f-f876-58cc-9f27-8c5979268f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{({x}^{4}-8x)}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535457} {:answer/id #uuid "4de03e91-0e5b-5d78-b5c9-5f081a9cc132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({[8x+1]}^{2}-{[{x}^{4}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535550} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc93862-a6af-52a4-8d50-916919b94dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{(1+8x-{x}^{4})}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535637} {:answer/id #uuid "bd28678f-f932-5c98-b9b4-933262b0cff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({[1-8x]}^{2}-{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd28678f-f932-5c98-b9b4-933262b0cff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({[1-8x]}^{2}-{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535724} :answer-field/id #uuid "2653d026-3ae7-59aa-9fa9-e9cc01abc497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5e827a7-4590-5f6c-9029-38608edd38d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124601" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dccfcb991a6c302c522ea73381544d2e6f93b7310f54040151a00832473596b8.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=8x$ and $y=-{x}^{4}$ for $x\\in [-2,0]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/33/335b300d66c7a51a37d595f5fb99a281c00ec6a5be62e1be12c2a3104cd54694.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-{x}^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=8x.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=8x.$\n\n![](images/aa/aae56ea01faa2c09f004fe2506c036f4d5e5b2e591e2e653f4794e4a3cecc309.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(1)-(8x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1-8x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-{x}^{4}.$\n\n![](images/63/635d3aabdc16fd90c2e2a06a042f98abe5dc16f1d7747394cd9083858023adee.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(1)-(-{x}^{4})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1-8x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({[1-8x]}^{2}-{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5bcc35-60c7-54bb-b133-39f6fc654277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535525} {:answer/id #uuid "67e852b0-b9c4-54a2-b205-8a42f1866dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535598} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1eabee-f679-5494-9115-f00cebaa9bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535683} {:answer/id #uuid "e77f7513-a64c-53f2-b5d3-d5040243a753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535791} {:answer/id #uuid "eb88f1de-5cdb-542c-9488-e6ab9bd38152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d1eabee-f679-5494-9115-f00cebaa9bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535683} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5160fe-ce43-5a25-8b6a-5f217be58058" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25934858-d636-5a89-8459-35c28b65815f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124728" :question/problem "![](images/ef/efe8084c2e5b9d5eff4f75dffc9acce77aa66ab0dc62c699589573d96f884d49.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-{x}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/45/4514019be21deb238c92f557cc041aec9b5e6629bc9d7dcac133a7aa2aed89c7.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={x}^{2}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/61/611d10f83c76467c69a1510809738611962dd5d1e1c22a10b0cb3081d2aa52cc.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(-{x}^{2})-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={x}^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/68/683244c0b5be598da85a8875708f459d4f87319677111f1072c997d911075d68.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,({x}^{2}-2)-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}((2-{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4-4{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4-4{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(4x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ((4(1)-\\frac{4(1)^{3}}{3})-(4(-1)-\\frac{4(-1)^{3}}{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi ((4-\\frac{4}{3})-(-4+\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi (8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0538fe89-8dc3-52a1-ae06-27b01e1b4965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535445} {:answer/id #uuid "55d1530f-3fa2-5a08-8bcc-98aa2c0fd192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535569} {:answer/id #uuid "972bf55d-1dab-510d-9213-5a9e7ecde914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3})-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535672} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aca940-e7f6-56a7-a52f-f3fc13aea2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3})-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535784} {:answer/id #uuid "fc85b5f0-dcd6-58f0-b09d-4cc19f8fcf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0538fe89-8dc3-52a1-ae06-27b01e1b4965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535445} :answer-field/id #uuid "58834ca7-0094-5902-ba0d-d4e72392d1de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6be7f528-8a2e-5e94-a08f-bb7c33bc8c26" :question/math-academy-id "q-125400" :question/problem "![](images/90/90980bb04eb7dd9906ec9a99ea3a1b7cfd9c00969bbd9b12c60fe0f80eb88840.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+2{y}^{2}=1$ and $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3})$ for $y\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equation of our first curve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2{y}^{2} & =1\\,⟹\\,x=1-2{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/16/16ca1c96786eb8e7ef12608f70d03b34b679e63d34ef41135b4fd6801bcccc27.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}).$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}).$\n\n![](images/27/276dea7b2d9f50e3fe171cceb64c1c7a12f84cfb18bdbe242102802ad9790f49.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}))\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3})]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c6fb140bb3eaf4c49cba76af157fb46a568bc2dc24e4763facede62720f844.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(1-2{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[2{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}))}^{2}-{(2{y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332e8fa1-bd6c-5b72-aab3-9a26d37f05ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({({y}^{2}+1)}^{2}-({y}^{2}+2y)^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535510} {:answer/id #uuid "69f76a16-6d32-5498-a766-01193d404013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}-({y}^{2}+2y-3)^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535601} {:answer/id #uuid "9144318b-29dd-55f5-a1c7-a01c036ef165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({({y}^{2}+1)}^{2}-(y+1)^{4})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535665} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d4c6ac-3f98-58f5-97d2-019d71e33c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}-(y+1)^{4})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535735} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c4ac8d-b33d-560a-bbe6-e5a6e34bfe53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}-({y}^{2}+1)^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7d4c6ac-3f98-58f5-97d2-019d71e33c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}-(y+1)^{4})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535735} :answer-field/id #uuid "9935c7d0-33f5-5372-8a86-7122b4fba33c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2f8487e-855b-5e52-8eac-210ae0123755" :question/math-academy-id "q-125546" :question/problem "![](images/55/559548dc5f2570d1341760ab87a2619646ed6985fd6437cb29442dc58bc6fee4.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+2y-1$ and $x=-1-{y}^{2}$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y-1 & =-1-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=0.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8f/8f1bccafe64a6926bdb214410453cecafa7059f74a539b155a4a4e6cccd67d1a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2y-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=-1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=-1-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d1d1ed82fadf105997e00d39b99992cdeba1d41be13cf599db63b34902e149a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(-1-{y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2y-1.$\n\n![](images/04/040e0a87b4bb5c7121a27fd2cc94c520f76919afbb86a49cfed2ed0cf57c1561.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,({y}^{2}+2y-1)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y-1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}-{[(y+1)^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}-(y+1)^{4})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf122b0-09c1-5956-8744-d8096ee07f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535580} {:answer/id #uuid "72635bb1-6cf3-55f9-b41a-e40454b6226a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535612} {:answer/id #uuid "7703febf-5ebf-5006-8d19-7494902439a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535617} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dc44a6-ce5e-5c08-a960-f7295a035378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535715} {:answer/id #uuid "d05b1475-6c63-5ec8-b5a9-e873d5d9039f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6dc44a6-ce5e-5c08-a960-f7295a035378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535715} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce1cd99e-e545-5f30-9775-38b6fad7af7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88a251ee-6143-5e86-bf44-46f8b33042cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-125555" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac7f02919896aeb40859b1d78608ec7620c26ea77becc806a28d39ef8abacd12.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ for $y\\in [1,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/76/76196a70cdbc65d2f91b4ba500ea83083be5cfe10fe3f3bd09c035e3ea7bcfba.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/e5/e5d8affefbde216fed0bf6acd7d464e921c1cf04e2578050d12c928d6dee5bd0.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3)-(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4002c7922eac8e7c97d402833eabc3621ed4da2d13799d07206a45cf0afd1f1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3)-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e428057-03fb-5069-bc10-8f485b235085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535462} {:answer/id #uuid "22a2ea8b-dab7-5699-8e84-0ecaba7bd180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}-{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535490} {:answer/id #uuid "b604d99c-c5fb-5b46-b2b9-bfe266992e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}-{[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535713} {:answer/id #uuid "ba912d5b-c16a-5c98-8772-74fb6eb18f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535719} {:answer/id #uuid "de2ba3e0-ce17-5d40-841f-829521df6752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}+{5}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e428057-03fb-5069-bc10-8f485b235085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535462} :answer-field/id #uuid "279f0e25-70bd-5ea5-9539-e50f5ef9c3b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "923c9707-06d1-5031-b8df-221476502c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-30045" :question/problem "![](images/43/43cff74165732892223841f6780e7aa22c024e3b3c173e60ce15d8d6841a5a08.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x$ for $x\\in [-2,\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7a/7aad98fe0513514aa8827bea4e9ffbac5b70bcb8da7923effd789988c81e286f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}-1.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}-1.$\n\n![](images/62/623b59b7aca8b33c6149ac6b0916d1eeb425adc3f74568868383806cd9b03b12.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(5)-({x}^{2}-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/c0/c0467a3bf02287589f897586a1358e4abb02759134b6b58622125319dc7331da.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[\\,(5)-(-\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4143ee0b-f61f-5bed-8dea-57c109e9d3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})\\,\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535535} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1f436d-ae2c-50df-a1f4-1d944523dcf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535583} {:answer/id #uuid "70798353-c69b-5617-a2cd-3fa3a1c92bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}-{[2-x]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535605} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfc17f0-061c-50c1-9e66-8930961ad9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[2-x]}^{2}-{[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535661} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a2ce51-d59b-5b33-9751-62df0da2f6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70798353-c69b-5617-a2cd-3fa3a1c92bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}-{[2-x]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535605} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b388436-18ed-5b93-906f-1dc37334a242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfc9867e-24b3-5baa-adb5-18feba56d583" :question/math-academy-id "q-30046" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2a8da9dd6d088d30b0b023d50ebf2c23e2bcfda89cd390ec16779694ae5eb39.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=x$ in the first quadrant is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =x \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/82/82783afd970ec3f0e90a8b97a12207b0ed8cbf82323e0512e9fe36b99a6fdc2f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{3}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{3}.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a9a72a194226f73da234e03b93e4b255849315808ac948c0958d8dee52b4f73.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-({x}^{3})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=x.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c53b4b1ad169119cadf490636b12235b3fc225a08d57c11d4f8e26318797f25.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-(x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}-{[2-x]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c41717b-c907-5031-97ec-c6ceee1cef10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535501} {:answer/id #uuid "662f9b8b-3b6f-5917-96ff-e6a29123f219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[7-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535596} {:answer/id #uuid "79083731-d85c-58a3-ad1e-660ad2fcee5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{3/5}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x-3)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535620} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2b46ce-9749-5847-8d8e-c71feca07989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[7-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[5+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535725} {:answer/id #uuid "ef590c46-ffb7-51f4-b8a4-b0f49dc57c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}+{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c41717b-c907-5031-97ec-c6ceee1cef10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535501} :answer-field/id #uuid "527a8b8b-3d1f-5a1f-9fab-9a12c4893e26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2e19710-89b7-5129-86a4-6f0be5f110ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-30047" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf2c500974f9c88751b9dec98cc53b5d3616b736f3ab0504adae5879cac2001a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}-2x$ and $y=3-3{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-\\frac{3}{5},1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d5/d5593e8c1e1d544acb8824d9428823d3d6cef3971a1cedd4b087a7902b0e2239.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=3-3{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\n![](images/bd/bddc78f683e28ec6e97fddb32f95dfdaaff27d3aafbdfc61f424857916f81a6c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(5)-(2{x}^{2}-2x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=3-3{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc049c46593baf1d96c04b95680d6b444ab9ceffc64d1f23f310f70e78684c14.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(5)-(3-3{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2230d1f1-b787-5319-b1e0-905614128e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535487} {:answer/id #uuid "2feedb22-02f6-5c03-b5c0-72b912429a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535504} {:answer/id #uuid "35b86732-8c4d-5186-99cd-0728f2b353c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535516} {:answer/id #uuid "638ef772-3dd4-513c-9881-1e7ac959700e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535591} {:answer/id #uuid "84a59b81-e90b-51c8-b019-cc66c59fb932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84a59b81-e90b-51c8-b019-cc66c59fb932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535645} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5fce8d-1e83-5220-b88b-1bc28cf4c002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89556ba4-a6e5-533a-b6c1-5971b5251496" :question/math-academy-id "q-30118" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c62793a0dc7ff37a4d6b7cf157239f21e6b0cdef8c3d428916f074584462598b.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=3x$ for $y\\in [0,9]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=5.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =3x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/bd/bd75a7448cfda8237a403f4ea67a99c38e41788839f5c88b89487106e49547d3.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}y.$\n\n![](images/0e/0ec13c4447954916af6e3b72abffefb69a46a12671b7b8388a873835654ec921.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}{[\\,(5)-(\\frac{1}{3}y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}{[5-\\frac{1}{3}y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n![](images/74/7404d889d1388540821bbd282f82322e3249534b8d4518a6236e9f4bf3164d2c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}[\\,(5)-(\\sqrt{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}[5-\\sqrt{y}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}({(5-\\frac{1}{3}y)}^{2}-{(5-\\sqrt{y})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{9}(\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}y+10\\sqrt{y})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{20{y}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{13{y}^{2}}{6})|}_{0}^{9} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{20\\cdot {9}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{9}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{13\\cdot {9}^{2}}{6})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{63\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71023dc7-345b-555a-9dcd-8f0cc9edde60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535606} {:answer/id #uuid "89f357c1-9ec0-5f4e-843e-66f2637b7692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535655} {:answer/id #uuid "d13c2ea6-e2b8-5f23-9a3b-c6edfff2ccc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535753} {:answer/id #uuid "d16d1940-8eb2-5e72-8e92-d1c1059e8cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535754} {:answer/id #uuid "d59f02f9-9c54-5daa-ae2f-984c80444c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f357c1-9ec0-5f4e-843e-66f2637b7692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535655} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b658f2-ac3d-5bc5-8869-0fed4b76530f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ae9fb77-cc16-5042-8460-37410a7d25d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-3385" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8d2665ab162ff21e358a80a6e42659b0385244a08d94f8de3ad5df12b434a9a.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}({x}^{3/2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7a/7aaa03f21130529bdf5572acac29bfe7566724d8acacc99ca5fc0b035de0398b.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/a7/a7bd5b7f8e61be991cb6a54e9b1277bd6df4fd874dbd72ec90802b4f957c31bd.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-({x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/ee/ee93f00e178fa7fd7b8702538298a15a5c98d94205deeb71401a49d8dd891545.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(\\sqrt{x})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}((1-{x}^{2})^{2}-(1-\\sqrt{x})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-x+2\\sqrt{x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}\\,{x}^{5}-\\frac{2}{3}\\,{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}\\,{x}^{3/2}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ((\\frac{1}{5}\\,(1)^{5}-\\frac{2}{3}\\,(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,(1)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}\\,(1)^{3/2})-(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{11}{30}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9e9c0db-7f76-520c-aaea-771c467b95be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2048\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e9c0db-7f76-520c-aaea-771c467b95be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2048\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535821} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6f2fe1-3336-5fe7-90da-fba93d12c56d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92412be4-49e1-54ee-8609-445cb6da4a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-348267" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc70e0f7c7ac82e399e520faa39cf04d252501da46834e88c06d8eba2b8d192d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=16$ and $x+4y=16$ for $y\\in [0,4]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=16.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =16\\,⟹\\,x=16-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+4y & =16\\,⟹\\,x=16-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f3/f39f9f399ea4d0e65a499f34d41158ddbaf5319a02e057e097b4f80c8197ba9e.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=16-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=16-4y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=16-4y.$\n\n![](images/47/47d985510b37b7a14e82dfc20bc136694e7ba2e17172b35be45ebde88d238677.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\,(16)-(16-4y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[4y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=16-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e5494005010f3cfb8fb8b116c4b8ca91e8d3bf2712ad45c60f0766a2021b19a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(16)-(16-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(4y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(16{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{16{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{16(4)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(4)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1024}{3}-\\frac{1024}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2048\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0f95781-af63-5159-8189-bcf05ccc46f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1296\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0f95781-af63-5159-8189-bcf05ccc46f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1296\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535751} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0bf9425-3265-53af-a7f4-242c609c9e7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b034c5b9-fc9c-52d3-a856-d07f183d8ea8" :question/math-academy-id "q-350914" :question/problem "![](images/80/8063ba9fbe4c569519eea27e5a5cd69a8f0e708b03d153942fbf22e9345af96a.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}+2$ and $y=20-{x}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=20.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =20-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f1/f196ef4a3b29a9d62609b0a9290f34d2cb1ef1351baa4883441fd89e9345e4ed.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=20-{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}+2.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](images/c0/c006e66d82e0059b341520bebe983fa75ed144e3050ca8c4493a4b6df3e8f352.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(20)-({x}^{2}+2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[18-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=20-{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c91a8e5ed0881ed0c8f34fa2ed6ccb5f95ff75a2f3b48e2d340f8a8856885e3a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(20)-(20-{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[18-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}((18-{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}(324-36{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}(324-36{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(324x-12{x}^{3})|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\pi ((324(3)-12(3)^{3})-(324(-3)-12(-3)^{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi ((972-324)-(-972+324)) \\\\  & =\\pi (648-(-648)) \\\\  & =1296\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f83b0c08-5ea8-5827-be29-e60a8c51de64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1250\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f83b0c08-5ea8-5827-be29-e60a8c51de64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1250\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535815} :answer-field/id #uuid "10353d36-c2a6-5baa-bc0e-12fb4776c147" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c99954c-8e68-5e26-befe-7d930f5b079f" :question/math-academy-id "q-351346" :question/problem "![](images/64/64b0f84b62adc0db3385a75d8fdc6e6707eb6778084b9e21de863ca6954c4c1d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=25$ and $x+5y=25$ for $y\\in [0,5]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=25.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+5y & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b7cafc0ad6ab7624c1274451f16ba2c3c9a111ac9dad3abd90f082cc42d9b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\n![](images/16/166dbb3f75000dfc155abfc3452ff6874dab1542409edc7e4867c5a5ee73c0b0.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[\\,(25)-(25-5y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[5y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/93/9322b6f4bc9a0e913670f8dcbc9657206c3ae6a30de2f26308054d75b67d0861.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(25)-(25-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}({(5y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}(25{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{25{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{25(5)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(5)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3125}{3}-625) \\\\  & =\\frac{1250\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f9d099-d483-540e-9e95-fcbce5c20534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4f9d099-d483-540e-9e95-fcbce5c20534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535693} :answer-field/id #uuid "32684ee3-c288-58d9-93e1-88b30f665d28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d651054f-0a78-573d-a5b9-95d84b459c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-361808" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a242a5bec4be4cb58b77289ea2ccf49bf22a368c19ec18f10790a417bfbff72.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=3-x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=4.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/25/255abcfb1a9034df297dc4939d945d8c3b1c4bd717cebde2844aa2a229fd5258.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=3-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b480a3bb7312a59edcabb133100bee1ebd5777664ea490b58744171e05e3083.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(4)-({x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=3-x.$\n\n![](images/20/20de93738463a5b04c0c5c0884d1db7d8867503931122c99a27fc82493b64edb.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(4)-(3-x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(4-{x}^{2})}^{2}-{(1+x)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}-2x+15)\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+15x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}(1)^{5}-3(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+15(1))-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}-3-1+15) \\\\  & =\\pi (11+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{55}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5a170f-7065-5de8-8d67-32ce20245ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c5a170f-7065-5de8-8d67-32ce20245ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535603} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cd8ab93-f2fe-5eea-85cd-ce5fd45592c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d03566e-e00c-5038-a04e-9a446a86a195" :question/math-academy-id "q-361829" :question/problem "![](images/77/778041d84c40044d6f047203a017bd64c0569bcfc9442afca9baa5160b494ae7.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+1 & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =x \\\\ x & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=4.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7c/7ce02bca7667a9c32971b7840ff2ba4b96ef3a5c9b18aceaeb5b9b713cc7b36f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\n![](images/6c/6cb557a57a189a426b6cccacd599562332b05aace5bbbfc5d996b0d53fed3359.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\sqrt{x}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/26/26886afd61251cae273c086dba59dcfa912665cdb788b0c3ef866bf61907efa2.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\frac{x}{2}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(\\sqrt{x}+2)}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{2}+2)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x+4\\sqrt{x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{12}\\,{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,{x}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi ((-\\frac{1}{12}\\,(4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,(4)^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,(4)^{3/2})-(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (8) \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13772a60-d9e6-519e-81ff-a87b69705c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[\\frac{5y}{8}+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535468} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1973fe-fa48-5311-9b7e-e2acae1001af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}({[\\frac{5y}{8}]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535477} {:answer/id #uuid "4f48cd37-a024-5a0c-b78d-161e36a73060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}({[\\frac{5y}{8}+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535553} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0443d6-5de1-5cf6-a9fa-57df36d5b4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5y}{8}+2]}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535678} {:answer/id #uuid "c18caf2a-05e8-5f0e-acf6-74e8d8e57477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}([\\frac{5y}{8}+2]-[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2])\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f48cd37-a024-5a0c-b78d-161e36a73060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}({[\\frac{5y}{8}+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535553} :answer-field/id #uuid "a538906d-e42b-5de1-87a1-cf764b3258be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd731e5d-ee05-5b06-811e-7a9b6ae9709a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49945" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c22369a6f1abc89437de1977135208ef5d0e762a4971df1c9ace14b6af932cd.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{5y}{8}$ and $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{5y}{8} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =5y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-5y & =0 \\\\ y(2y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/50/504e204d73a1f87d72a321e7c0f58eadf01840ff3b8dbc5214f08e50fcbdcf9b.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{8}y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{8}y.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c43a01a8548112d8dc1f30f9059c560ac7bec1e679a6c76394d83e4bfbea668.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\,(\\frac{5}{8}y)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8478ef13983e6529fa10147f9659171e31a5555cb799ec1fad13d1fe8b25191.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\,(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}({[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1ee210-e8c3-5b0b-b961-e2ea6bb04afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535464} {:answer/id #uuid "378a3f70-b83c-543f-8230-8a9c470b7cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[\\frac{5}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535519} {:answer/id #uuid "60fcac8a-9636-51dd-b16e-559340914a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535586} {:answer/id #uuid "7e880303-2fe8-5c05-b664-1056f270eb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535634} {:answer/id #uuid "9907a464-6ae3-5500-b762-ac9746b04080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60fcac8a-9636-51dd-b16e-559340914a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535586} :answer-field/id #uuid "7988a85f-c1d9-5415-bd21-c53e94524cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ad0e9c9-a63e-55c4-a91b-b9b32de9ed5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-50324" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0e8cbe1a26b5a012f6098b67320070e882fa38783d8eda07dd0b76011a47b21.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5}{2}y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-\\frac{5}{2}y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-5y+2 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(2y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2},2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ to $y=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d5/d5067706f9ff1abbdfd9dfce94711cb854f79c1156ddc0d81c1c35a5ed555f7a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y.$\n\n![](images/a9/a944979bc352b0e40afea73836b22c1fb1513fabbc727fb60d4c96793ee6fbb5.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{5}{2}-y)-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1eb04cf2f3ae0669dd82106b064dd822da618e9ca31bb3b96e4e30c71b7c3193.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{1}{y})-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504eada9-0247-54fc-b6dc-cbc0f935842b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535556} {:answer/id #uuid "8af8d130-ce97-50dd-8f18-515090d74071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535657} {:answer/id #uuid "8bff95c5-eea5-547b-9c2a-215dee3b9aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535658} {:answer/id #uuid "9ace08dc-6b71-5df3-a9ac-066f0a3dea8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535680} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe67cfc-1a30-5c73-9315-623a9704cafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bff95c5-eea5-547b-9c2a-215dee3b9aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535658} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f225294-fdb8-504b-93be-81ef45509833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc2bc671-1b50-5524-a44c-d196ad9a937f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85687" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0aa7d362596dcea976fead0467b9eb76c04b85c02c55dbd12e9a95652eda276.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ for $y\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x & \\,⟹\\,x=y \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f1/f15165acc18f4cf828671e3f9085274b26598a643be9eeb97be617272d7008d7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/54/54820b3b386c9a435de04e0b3f07d4e2221a3e026ba5ba93ca25fa6640aa0ab1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e1a6067500f81cabeac8dc43c4c585da608ea655667dc5e00f29a6fad0dda60.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[3-y]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(3-{y}^{2})}^{2}-{(3-y)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{4}-7{y}^{2}+6y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7{y}^{3}}{3}+3{y}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot {1}^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0769fe07-7b97-54d3-9da7-2e16745b3d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535450} {:answer/id #uuid "09466455-4240-5580-81c8-13e20c58266d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{112\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535451} {:answer/id #uuid "21bd23c7-f81d-5056-b97e-1aaed7cf5025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535485} {:answer/id #uuid "300fc692-4366-5f31-b23d-7017bcadb5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535505} {:answer/id #uuid "d607b83b-6442-5533-98d0-d815d6fc8ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09466455-4240-5580-81c8-13e20c58266d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{112\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535451} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f68768-c4e2-51d6-ae21-d67ca3a8019c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db9dcd58-6ee1-5525-a1ac-d82114a9c0f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-86194" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6ba207cd34725ec2e3995eb0f90e63bedc8573300335b2d31163457ef8ee9bad.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+1$ and $x=2{y}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =-1,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/41/41f8dff0676c5cf8952f46b174ff4f98deeb97eec272e417f145b4782770c156.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4d3d75dfab4aa6382bf47f06a57036ec612a05c90d0764cd48fdc1f150f2453.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,({y}^{2}+1)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/6f/6f79c95e2b2d3aaf71e6dfac5ca93fa20261f5f40e1660cefc4ac23af3e56cf8.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,(2{y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2{y}^{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[{y}^{2}+3]}^{2}-{[2{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3{y}^{4}-2{y}^{2}+5)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{3{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+5y)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+5\\cdot 1)-(-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{3}}{3}+5\\cdot (-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{112\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
